# Per-lead /next/<slug> pages (added 2026-09-03).
# A lead file carries a minor's first name, a parent's email address and phone
# number, and the answers they typed into an intake form. THIS REPOSITORY IS
# PUBLIC, and the proposal generator's per-family records are gitignored for
# exactly this reason. The route reads this directory at build time
# (app/next/[slug]/leads.js), so the mechanism lives in git and the families do
# not. The example file is committed so the route stays buildable and reviewable
# on a fresh clone.
/app/next/leads/*.json
# The `*.json` pattern above does NOT match `charles.json.bak-2026-09-09`, and a
# .bak beside the file is how every edit in this lane is made reversible. So the
# backups are named explicitly (2026-09-09, first one written); without this line
# an `add -A` from any lane commits a parent's email address to a public repo.
/app/next/leads/*.json.bak*
# And the same hole exists for EVERY other suffix, which cost a near-miss on
# 2026-09-09: two retired rows renamed to `*.json.retired-<date>` appeared as
# untracked files carrying a minor's first name in a public repo, because the two
# patterns above only cover the bare name and `.bak`. This catches anything that
# starts as a lead file whatever it is renamed to. The retired pair was moved out
# of the repo entirely rather than relying on this line; the line is for the next
# rename nobody thinks about.
/app/next/leads/*.json.*
!/app/next/leads/example-*.json
